{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.TraceReadingsDiffer","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TraceReadingsDiffer","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.CharZero.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.readings_not_related_of_invariance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.readings_not_related_of_invariance","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L103-L122","doc":"**Reading (A) is refuted, conditional on the invariance hypothesis.**\n\nGiven any relation `R` on a type of algebra elements whose trace `τ` is\n`R`-invariant — Markov equivalence and the Markov trace being the instance\nintended — two elements whose traces differ are not `R`-related.  Applied to the\nproton atom element and `β_Bor`, whose traces differ by the theorems above, this\nsays they are not Markov equivalent, so they do not name a common `K`.\n\nThe invariance is a **hypothesis**, not a claim proved here; see the module\ndocstring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.not_rel_of_invariant_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.not_rel_of_invariant_ne","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L95-L101","doc":"**An invariant separates.**  If `τ` takes equal values on every `R`-related\npair, then a difference in `τ` forbids the relation.  Stated for an arbitrary\nrelation so it applies to Markov equivalence without that notion being\nformalised here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trM_readings_differ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trM_readings_differ","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L89-L93","doc":"Hence the two readings are distinct as functions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trM_readings_differ_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trM_readings_differ_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L82-L87","doc":"**The refutation.** The two readings are not the same function of `q`; they\nalready differ at `q = 1`, where both denominators are nonzero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trMProtonAtom_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trMProtonAtom_one","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L77-L80","doc":"At the classical limit the atom-element reading is `1/2` — a factor of two\nbelow the Borromean one, and the separation is already visible here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trMBorromean_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trMBorromean_one","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L73-L75","doc":"At the classical limit the Borromean reading is `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trMProtonAtom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trMProtonAtom","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L65-L71","doc":"Markov trace of the canonical proton **atom element** — the object\n`atom_braid_word_3A(1,0)` returns, which lies in `H₃(q)` outside the image of\n`ρ : B₃ → H₃(q)` because `2A+1` of its letters are weight-1 averages over\ncrossing sign.  This is what production loads as `ρ(proton)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.trMBorromean","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.trMBorromean","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TraceReadingsDiffer.lean#L59-L63","doc":"Markov trace of the Borromean braid `(σ₁σ₂⁻¹)³` in `H₃(q)`, convention D\n(`z = q³/(q²+1)`), as a rational function of `q`.  Coefficients from\n`markov_trace_canonical.tr_M_element`; see the module docstring on provenance. ","signatureChunk":0}]}